From: ed.leslie@canremote.uucp (ED LESLIE)
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
Subject: Print thru print port
Message-ID: <735250e56c2d267f2a6c@canremote.uucp>
Date: 20 Jun 90 05:49:00 GMT
Organization: Canada Remote Systems Limited, Mississauga, ON, Canada
Lines: 41
Posted: Wed Jun 20 06:49:00 1990

heuer@decus.com.au (Powell A HEUER) writes:
hu>We have a number TSX-Plus (a multi-user PDP-11 operating system)
hu>systems, each of them with a number of VT-series terminals with
hu>printer ports.  Some of these terminals are fairly widely dispersed
hu>so it takes a lot of serial lines to provided printers convenient to
hu>all users. However, a printer connected to the printer port of the
hu>user's terminal would be a good solution in many cases.

hu>Does anyone know of software, ideally a pseudo-device handler, that
hu>allows a printer on the VT-terminal's printer port to be used as the
hu>destination for that user's printer output?

I don't know about TSX-Plus code, but the print spooler on RSX-11M/M+ is
fairly easily modified to do just this. I have done it on several
client's machines. The RSX print despooler (this is the portion of the
software which actually outputs to the device) has functionality to
'share' a non-dedicated printer. In this mode, it attaches the printer
at the start of a job, and detaches at the end of the job. It is fairly
easy to insert code into the same locations to output the escape
sequence to turn on and off transparent printing (the output goes to the
printer port, but not to the screen). This means that your CRT line is
busy until the print finishes -- but my client's prints are generally
short, and they installed 256KB print buffers in-line with the printers.

The same method could, of course, be extended to grab the printer port
on a line by line basis.

The next problem encountered was that RSX allows only 16 queues, which
is a severe limitation. I hacked at it some more, and came up with a mod
such that the 8 despoolers (called PP0 through PP7 for this client) look
at system data structures to see what terminal port queued the output.
Thus, the system can support up to 8 simultaneous printer port outputs
to an indefinite number of CRT users. Since the client uses only this
method of driving printers, the default queue is directed to the set of
PPx's.

Hope this has been of assistance.

Regards....Ed Leslie
---
 


