We are running Process Control's ftp on an RT-11 system, a TSX+ system
and until recently a VMS system.  We have been generally satisfied with
their performance (at least since version 2.0 of the TSX+ software
became available).  The software is certainly expensive, which is why
we're only running ftp and not telnet or smtp.  There are none of the
more sophisticated tools available, such as dns or nfs.  The tie of the
software to a board serial number is also very inconvenient - our VMS
system went through a series of reconfigurations, and we seemed to be
calling PC's representatives monthly for new passwords!  I am also
concerned about the position if (for instance) Process Software went
bust - how would we get a new password if we changed the configuration,
perhaps following a maintenance repair by DEC?

Version 1.x of the TSX+ ftp drove a coach and horses through any
pretence of security on the system - anybody on the network could access
the machine.  Version 2.0 fixes that, but still has security weaknesses
which are sufficiently severe that we are not willing to license one of
our machines which we would otherwise like to connect  to our network -
although a valid user id and password are required to open a connection,
once connected, there are no access restrictions once a user has opened
that connection.  My grumbles on this are well known to Process.

All of those qualifications aside, Process seem to be the only game in
town.  They can be reached on (US) 413 549 6994 fax 413 549 5273.

Russ Evans
British Geological Survey, Edinburgh

